"""Generated by make.py: (module, kind, question, model answer)."""
Q = [('1.1', 'Recall', 'Define scientific machine learning in one sentence.', 'Machine learning that combines data with scientific knowledge such as physical laws, to build models that are accurate, general and trustworthy.'), ('1.1', 'Explain', 'Why does a pure data model fail when extrapolating?', 'It only learned patterns inside the range of its data and has no built-in reason to behave sensibly outside it.'), ('1.1', 'Explain', 'What extra demands does Physical AI face compared with software AI?', 'It must respect physics, act in real time, and be safe when wrong, because its actions have physical consequences.'), ('1.1', 'Recall', 'Name three families of SciML methods.', 'Physics-informed neural networks, equation discovery, operator learning and surrogates, hybrid models, and uncertainty quantification.'), ('1.1', 'Apply', 'Give a transport example where data are sparse where it matters.', 'Crashes, extreme crowd densities, rare congestion events, or future climate and policy scenarios.'), ('1.1', 'Apply', 'When might a pure physics model be enough?', 'When the laws are well known and parameters are measurable, such as projectile motion or simple heat flow, and details beyond the model do not matter.'), ('1.2', 'Recall', 'What does a differential equation tell you?', 'How fast a quantity changes, as a function of its current state (and sometimes time).'), ('1.2', 'Calculate', 'Decay with k = 0.5, y0 = 1, one Euler step of 0.5 s. What is y1?', 'y1 = 1 + 0.5 × (−0.5 × 1) = 0.75.'), ('1.2', 'Explain', "Why can Euler's method blow up?", 'It extrapolates along the current slope; with large steps or stiff/oscillatory problems the errors grow each step instead of shrinking.'), ('1.2', 'Recall', 'What is the difference between an ODE and a PDE?', 'An ODE has one independent variable (usually time); a PDE has several, such as space and time.'), ('1.2', 'Apply', 'Give a transport example of a PDE.', 'The LWR traffic-flow model, air pollution dispersion, or heat in pavements.'), ('1.2', 'Apply', 'How do you check a numerical solution is accurate enough?', 'Halve the step size; if the answer barely changes, the solution has converged.'), ('1.3', 'Recall', 'Write the computation of one tanh neuron.', 'a = tanh(w·x + b): multiply inputs by weights, add the bias, apply tanh.'), ('1.3', 'Explain', 'What is the universal approximation idea?', 'A network with enough hidden neurons can approximate any continuous function on a bounded range, by adding many soft steps.'), ('1.3', 'Calculate', 'Parameters of a 1–20–1 network with tanh hidden layer?', '3 × 20 + 1 = 61.'), ('1.3', 'Explain', 'Why do smooth networks struggle with sharp jumps?', 'Each neuron is a smooth soft step, so representing a sharp jump needs very steep or many neurons; otherwise the jump gets blurred.'), ('1.3', 'Apply', 'Name one way a network can break physics in transport.', 'Predicting negative speeds, flows above capacity, or vehicles appearing from nowhere.'), ('1.3', 'Recall', 'What three things does a network need to learn?', 'Training data, a loss function and an optimiser.'), ('1.4', 'Recall', 'Write the gradient descent update.', 'w ← w − η ∇L(w), with η the learning rate.'), ('1.4', 'Calculate', 'L = (w − 3)², w = 0, η = 0.1. Next w?', 'w = 0 − 0.1 × 2 × (0 − 3) = 0.6.'), ('1.4', 'Explain', 'Why might gradient descent stop in a poor solution?', 'It can settle in a local minimum, a valley that is not the deepest, because every nearby step goes uphill.'), ('1.4', 'Explain', 'What does momentum do?', 'It adds a fraction of the previous update to the current one, smoothing the path and helping to roll past small bumps.'), ('1.4', 'Explain', 'Why is automatic differentiation essential for physics-informed learning?', 'It gives exact derivatives of the network output with respect to inputs such as time and space, which the physics residual needs.'), ('1.4', 'Apply', 'Training loss is jumping up and down wildly. What do you try first?', 'Lower the learning rate (and check the data scaling).'), ('1.5', 'Recall', 'Write the two parts of a physics-informed loss.', 'A data misfit term at measurements plus λ times the squared equation residual at collocation points.'), ('1.5', 'Explain', 'Why are collocation points cheap?', 'They need no measurements: we only evaluate the model and its derivatives there, so we can place many anywhere.'), ('1.5', 'Explain', 'Why does the residual need automatic differentiation?', 'The residual contains derivatives of the model output with respect to inputs, such as dŷ/dt, which autodiff computes exactly.'), ('1.5', 'Apply', 'Your PINN fits the data but the residual stays large. What might you change?', 'Increase the physics weight, add collocation points, or rescale the loss terms.'), ('1.5', 'Explain', 'When is a classical solver better than a PINN?', 'For well-posed forward problems with known parameters and no data, where meshes and solvers are fast and accurate.'), ('1.5', 'Apply', 'Give a transport inverse problem.', 'Estimating the free-flow speed or jam density of a road from sparse detector data and the LWR model.'), ('1.6', 'Recall', 'Define underfitting and overfitting.', 'Underfitting: model too simple, misses the pattern. Overfitting: model too flexible, fits noise and fails on new data.'), ('1.6', 'Explain', 'Why does training error always fall as models get more flexible?', 'A more flexible model can always fit the training points at least as well, including their noise.'), ('1.6', 'Apply', 'Your traffic model has 2% training error and 30% test error. Diagnose.', 'Overfitting: simplify, regularise, add data or add physical constraints.'), ('1.6', 'Explain', 'What does regularisation add to the loss?', 'A penalty such as λ times the sum of squared weights, discouraging extreme values.'), ('1.6', 'Explain', 'Why is physics a good regulariser?', 'Real systems obey laws that forbid many shapes noise would create; penalising the residual removes them.'), ('1.6', 'Apply', 'How should you use a test set?', 'Keep it untouched until the end and use it once to report final performance.'), ('1.7', 'Recall', 'What is a surrogate model?', 'A fast approximate model trained on a few input–output pairs from an expensive model.'), ('1.7', 'Explain', 'When is building a surrogate worth it?', 'When each full run is expensive, many queries are needed, inputs are few and the response is fairly smooth.'), ('1.7', 'Explain', 'What does the shape parameter ε do in an RBF surrogate?', 'It sets how wide each bump is: too narrow dips between runs, too wide makes the system ill-conditioned.'), ('1.7', 'Apply', 'You can afford 10 GCAM runs over carbon prices 0–400 $/t. Where do you run them?', 'Spread evenly (or space-filling), then add runs adaptively where the surrogate is least certain.'), ('1.7', 'Recall', 'Name three kinds of surrogate.', 'Radial basis functions, Gaussian processes, polynomial chaos, neural networks.'), ('1.7', 'Explain', 'Why is surrogate uncertainty useful?', 'It shows where the surrogate may be wrong, guiding where to run the full model next and how much to trust answers.'), ('1.8', 'Recall', 'Name the four steps of the Physical AI loop.', 'Sense, estimate, decide, act.'), ('1.8', 'Explain', 'How does the Kalman filter combine model and measurement?', 'It predicts with the model, then moves the prediction towards the measurement by the Kalman gain, which depends on their relative uncertainties.'), ('1.8', 'Apply', 'Your filter follows every GPS jump. What do you change?', 'Trust the model more: reduce the assumed process noise q or increase the assumed measurement noise R.'), ('1.8', 'Explain', 'What is a digital twin?', 'A model of a real system that is continuously corrected with live data, used to monitor, forecast and test actions.'), ('1.8', 'Recall', 'Name one safety standard for automated vehicles.', 'ISO 26262 (functional safety) or ISO 21448 (SOTIF).'), ('1.8', 'Apply', 'Give a transport example of the sense–estimate–decide–act loop.', 'Adaptive cruise control, an adaptive traffic signal, or a lane-keeping system.'), ('2.1', 'Recall', 'List the seven steps of the SciML workflow.', 'Frame the question, gather data and physics, split honestly, build a baseline, add learning and physics, validate with uncertainty, document and share.'), ('2.1', 'Explain', 'Why does a random split over-estimate performance for time series?', 'Neighbouring hours are very similar, so the model effectively sees the answer for test hours through their neighbours in training.'), ('2.1', 'Apply', 'You normalise data using the mean of the whole data set before splitting. Problem?', "Yes: the test set's statistics leak into training. Fit normalisation on training data only."), ('2.1', 'Recall', 'Name two SciML libraries.', 'DeepXDE, NVIDIA PhysicsNeMo, NeuroMANCER, PySINDy.'), ('2.1', 'Explain', 'Why fix random seeds?', 'So training runs can be repeated exactly and differences can be attributed to changes you made.'), ('2.1', 'Apply', 'Propose a baseline for next-day hourly flow.', 'The same hour on the same weekday last week (seasonal persistence), or a physics/capacity model alone.'), ('2.2', 'Recall', 'List the six steps to build a PINN.', 'Network, collocation points, residual by autodiff, composite loss, training with Adam, and (optionally) a trainable parameter for the inverse problem.'), ('2.2', 'Explain', 'How do you compute dŷ/dt in PyTorch?', 'Set requires_grad on t, compute y = net(t), then torch.autograd.grad(y, t, ones_like(y), create_graph=True).'), ('2.2', 'Apply', 'Data loss is stuck high, physics loss tiny. Diagnose.', 'The physics weight is too large or the network too small; lower λ or enlarge the network.'), ('2.2', 'Explain', 'How does a PINN estimate k?', 'k is a trainable parameter; minimising data + residual loss finds the k under which the law and data agree.'), ('2.2', 'Explain', 'Why scale inputs and outputs to about ±1?', 'Tanh networks train best when inputs are order one; unscaled times or values make gradients badly balanced.'), ('2.2', 'Apply', 'When should you not use a PINN?', 'For a well-posed forward problem with known parameters where a classical solver is faster and more accurate.'), ('2.3', 'Recall', 'Write the explicit finite-difference update for the heat equation.', 'u_i ← u_i + r (u_{i+1} − 2u_i + u_{i−1}), with r = αΔt/Δx².'), ('2.3', 'Explain', 'What happens if r > 0.5?', 'High-frequency errors are amplified every step and the solution blows up.'), ('2.3', 'Calculate', 'Δx = 0.025, α = 1. Largest stable explicit Δt?', 'Δt ≤ 0.5 × 0.025² = 0.0003125.'), ('2.3', 'Explain', 'Why use finite volumes for traffic?', 'They conserve vehicles exactly and handle shock waves without spurious oscillations.'), ('2.3', 'Recall', 'What terms make up a PINN loss for the heat equation?', 'PDE residual at interior points, boundary-condition terms and an initial-condition term.'), ('2.3', 'Apply', 'When would you choose a PINN over finite differences for a PDE?', 'When fusing sparse data, inferring unknown parameters, or handling complex geometries without meshing.'), ('2.4', 'Recall', 'What does SINDy stand for?', 'Sparse Identification of Nonlinear Dynamics.'), ('2.4', 'Explain', 'Describe the STLSQ algorithm.', 'Least squares on all library terms, zero those below a threshold, refit the rest, repeat until stable.'), ('2.4', 'Apply', 'SINDy returns dv/dt = −4.0x − 0.3v + 0.02x³ + 0.01. What do you change?', 'Raise the threshold (and/or smooth the data) to remove small spurious terms, then check by simulation.'), ('2.4', 'Explain', 'Why is noise a particular problem for equation discovery?', 'Derivatives are estimated from differences divided by small time steps, which magnifies noise.'), ('2.4', 'Explain', 'When might you prefer a neural ODE to SINDy?', 'When the law is too complex for a small library or interpretability is less important than accuracy.'), ('2.4', 'Apply', 'Suggest candidate terms for discovering a car-following law.', 'Gap, relative speed, own speed, their products and squares, and terms like 1/gap.'), ('2.5', 'Recall', 'What is the difference between a function and an operator?', 'A function maps numbers to numbers; an operator maps a whole function to another function.'), ('2.5', 'Explain', 'Describe the DeepONet architecture.', 'A branch net encodes the input function sampled at sensors; a trunk net encodes the query location; their dot product gives the output.'), ('2.5', 'Explain', 'Why does operator learning pay off compared with PINNs for many scenarios?', 'A PINN is retrained for each input; a trained operator solves new inputs instantly.'), ('2.5', 'Apply', 'Your FNO works on training demand patterns but fails on a new event day. Why?', 'The event-day input is outside the training distribution; add similar examples or use physics-informed training.'), ('2.5', 'Recall', 'Name two neural operator architectures.', 'DeepONet and the Fourier neural operator.'), ('2.5', 'Apply', 'Suggest a transport operator-learning task.', 'Demand profile → congestion pattern on a corridor; emissions map → pollutant concentration.'), ('2.6', 'Recall', 'What two things does a GP prediction provide?', 'A mean (best estimate) and a variance (uncertainty) at every input.'), ('2.6', 'Explain', 'What does the kernel length scale control?', 'How far apart two inputs can be and still be considered similar, and hence how smooth the functions are.'), ('2.6', 'Explain', 'Why does GP variance not depend on observed y values?', 'With fixed hyperparameters, the posterior variance depends only on where observations are, not on their values.'), ('2.6', 'Apply', 'Your 95% bands cover only 60% of held-out points. Diagnose.', 'Overconfident: the length scale may be too long or the noise too small; re-tune by marginal likelihood or rethink the kernel.'), ('2.6', 'Recall', 'Name two uses of GPs in SciML.', 'Emulators of slow simulators, Bayesian calibration, Bayesian optimisation, kriging of sensor data.'), ('2.6', 'Explain', 'Why do GPs become slow for large data sets?', 'Exact inference needs a matrix factorisation costing order n³ operations.'), ('2.7', 'Recall', 'Write the form of a residual hybrid model.', 'y = physics(x) + ML(x), where ML is trained on data − physics.'), ('2.7', 'Explain', 'Why does a hybrid extrapolate better than pure ML?', 'Outside the data the small correction stays modest, so the prediction follows the physics, which is valid there.'), ('2.7', 'Apply', 'Your learned correction is as large as the physics prediction. What does this mean?', 'The physics model is badly wrong or mis-specified; revisit it before trusting the hybrid.'), ('2.7', 'Recall', 'Name three hybrid patterns.', 'Residual learning, parameter learning, closure learning (and gray-box models).'), ('2.7', 'Apply', 'Design a parameter-learning hybrid for road capacity.', 'A network predicts capacity and free-flow speed from weather, lighting and time; a traffic-flow model uses them.'), ('2.7', 'Explain', 'When might pure ML be fine instead?', 'When data are abundant, cover all conditions of interest, and extrapolation is not needed.'), ('2.8', 'Recall', 'List four kinds of evidence needed before deploying a SciML model.', 'Accuracy on unseen conditions, physical consistency, calibrated uncertainty, and a deployment plan with monitoring and fallbacks.'), ('2.8', 'Explain', 'Describe split conformal prediction.', 'Compute absolute errors on a calibration set, take the ⌈(n+1)(1−α)⌉-th smallest as q, and predict ŷ ± q.'), ('2.8', 'Explain', 'Why can conformal intervals under-cover some inputs?', 'The guarantee is averaged over inputs; with input-dependent noise, a constant band is too narrow where noise is large.'), ('2.8', 'Apply', "Your traffic model's intervals cover 90% overall but 70% at peak hours. Fix?", 'Use normalised scores or conformalised quantile regression, or calibrate peak hours separately.'), ('2.8', 'Recall', 'What is data drift?', 'A change in input or outcome distributions after deployment, which degrades model performance.'), ('2.8', 'Explain', 'Why publish a model card?', "So users know the model's intended use, training data, performance and where it must not be used."), ('3.1', 'Recall', 'What is traffic state estimation?', 'Estimating density, speed and flow along a whole road over time from sparse, noisy measurements.'), ('3.1', 'Explain', 'Why does physics help between detectors?', 'The LWR model propagates jams as waves at predictable speeds, so information moves correctly between sensors.'), ('3.1', 'Explain', 'What does the nudging gain control?', 'How strongly the model is pulled towards detector readings: too low ignores data, too high follows noise.'), ('3.1', 'Recall', 'Name two sources of traffic data.', 'Loop detectors, probe vehicles (GPS, connected vehicles), cameras, Bluetooth readers.'), ('3.1', 'Apply', 'Design a test for a new estimator on a motorway.', 'Use trajectory ground truth or simulation, hold out detectors, compare with interpolation and a Kalman baseline, report congested errors separately.'), ('3.1', 'Explain', 'Why are shock waves hard for PINN-type estimators?', 'Smooth networks struggle to represent sharp discontinuities (spectral bias), so fronts get smeared.'), ('3.2', 'Recall', 'Name the IDM parameters.', 'Desired speed v0, time gap T, minimum gap s0, maximum acceleration a, comfortable braking b.'), ('3.2', 'Explain', 'Why do human platoons amplify braking?', 'Reaction delays and over-reaction mean each follower brakes later and harder than the vehicle ahead.'), ('3.2', 'Explain', 'How does CACC improve string stability?', "It receives the leader's acceleration by radio and reacts immediately, before the gap changes."), ('3.2', 'Apply', 'What trade-off does a longer time gap bring?', 'Better stability and safety but lower road capacity (fewer vehicles per hour).'), ('3.2', 'Recall', 'Name a public trajectory dataset for car following.', 'NGSIM, highD, Waymo Open, Lyft Level 5.'), ('3.2', 'Explain', 'How can a neural network refine the IDM safely?', 'By learning a residual on top of IDM acceleration or predicting IDM parameters, so the physics still prevents collisions.'), ('3.3', 'Calculate', 'Gap 30 m, follower 20 m/s, leader 15 m/s. TTC?', '30 / (20 − 15) = 6 s.'), ('3.3', 'Calculate', 'DRAC for 8 m/s closing speed and 16 m gap?', '8² / (2 × 16) = 2 m/s².'), ('3.3', 'Explain', 'Why are conflicts useful surrogates for crashes?', 'They are much more frequent and physically related to crashes, so risk can be assessed in days rather than years.'), ('3.3', 'Explain', 'How does machine learning help conflict studies?', 'Detectors and trackers extract trajectories from video automatically, allowing TTC and PET to be computed for every interaction.'), ('3.3', 'Apply', 'How would you remove tracking artefacts from trajectories?', 'Physics-informed smoothing: constrain speeds and accelerations to physically possible values, then validate against manual annotations.'), ('3.3', 'Explain', 'Why must extreme value models be validated on crash data?', 'Tail extrapolation is sensitive to thresholds and assumptions and can be badly wrong.'), ('3.4', 'Recall', 'Write the road-load equation.', 'P = m v (a + g sinθ) + m g C_r v cosθ + ½ ρ C_d A v³.'), ('3.4', 'Calculate', 'Drag power at 20 m/s with ρ = 1.2, C_dA = 0.7?', '0.5 × 1.2 × 0.7 × 20³ = 3,360 W ≈ 3.4 kW.'), ('3.4', 'Explain', 'Why does an uphill grade raise emissions so much?', "Lifting the car's weight adds m g sinθ v to the power, comparable to or larger than rolling and drag at urban speeds."), ('3.4', 'Explain', 'What does the learned part of a hybrid emission model capture?', 'Effects the simple physics omits: load-dependent engine efficiency, cold starts, auxiliary loads.'), ('3.4', 'Apply', 'How would you estimate corridor CO₂ for a new speed limit?', 'Simulate traffic to get speed and acceleration profiles, apply the hybrid vehicle model per vehicle type, and sum with fleet shares and uncertainty.'), ('3.4', 'Explain', "Why does an EV's CO₂ per km depend on location?", 'Its emissions come from electricity generation, whose carbon intensity varies by grid and time.'), ('3.5', 'Recall', 'What does GCAM couple?', 'Energy, agriculture and land, water, the economy and the climate, across 32 regions.'), ('3.5', 'Explain', 'Why are emulators valuable for IAM policy studies?', 'Each full run is slow; emulators answer thousands of scenario and uncertainty queries in seconds.'), ('3.5', 'Explain', 'What is active learning in emulation?', "Iteratively choosing the next full-model run where the emulator's uncertainty is largest."), ('3.5', 'Apply', 'Your emulator is trained on carbon prices 0–200 $/t. Can you use it at 350 $/t?', 'No: it is only valid within the sampled range; run GCAM there first.'), ('3.5', 'Explain', 'Name two limitations of emulators.', "They inherit the model's structural assumptions and are only valid within sampled input ranges; multi-output consistency must be checked."), ('3.5', 'Apply', 'Which inputs might a GCAM transport emulator vary?', 'Carbon price, battery costs, fuel prices, biofuel availability, travel demand elasticities.'), ('3.6', 'Recall', "Write Weidmann's speed–density law.", 'v = v0 (1 − exp(−γ (1/ρ − 1/ρmax))), with v0 = 1.34 m/s, γ = 1.913, ρmax = 5.4 per m².'), ('3.6', 'Explain', 'Why does a free cubic fail at high density?', 'It is fitted only to low-density data and has no constraint forcing speed to fall to zero, so it can curve upwards.'), ('3.6', 'Explain', 'How can SciML improve social-force models?', 'By calibrating their parameters from trajectories and learning residual forces with networks while keeping the physical structure.'), ('3.6', 'Apply', 'Design a physics check for camera density maps.', 'Check that people are conserved between overlapping views and over time, given measured flows through boundaries.'), ('3.6', 'Explain', 'Name two privacy safeguards for crowd monitoring.', 'Aggregate densities instead of identities, on-site processing, minimal retention, transparency about purpose.'), ('3.6', 'Apply', 'Why validate a crowd model locally before a pilgrimage?', 'Walking speeds and behaviour vary with culture, age, purpose and setting, so imported parameters may mislead.'), ('3.7', 'Recall', 'What three components does a digital twin need?', 'A model of the system, live data, and a two-way link to decisions.'), ('3.7', 'Explain', 'How does data assimilation keep a twin accurate?', 'Each step, it predicts with the model and corrects the state towards new observations by a gain based on their uncertainties.'), ('3.7', 'Explain', 'Why can a physics-based twin survive sensor gaps?', 'Its model keeps predicting how the system evolves, so the estimate degrades gradually instead of disappearing.'), ('3.7', 'Apply', 'Name two uses of a corridor digital twin.', 'Forecasting congestion, testing signal plans or diversions, incident response, event planning.'), ('3.7', 'Explain', 'What happens with too high an assimilation gain and noisy detectors?', 'The twin follows detector noise, making its state and forecasts jumpy.'), ('3.7', 'Apply', 'How could learned surrogates help a city-scale twin?', 'They replace slow simulators with fast operator-learning or emulator models so forecasts and what-ifs run in real time.'), ('3.8', 'Recall', 'Name three open research fronts in SciML for transport.', 'Foundation and world models, verification of learned control, rare events, fairness and transfer.'), ('3.8', 'Explain', 'Why are rare events hard for machine learning?', 'There are almost no data on them, so models must rely on physics, simulation and careful extrapolation.'), ('3.8', 'Explain', 'What does a runtime safety monitor do?', "Checks a learned controller's actions online and switches to a safe fallback when limits might be violated."), ('3.8', 'Apply', 'List five items from the honest-reporting checklist.', 'Leakage-free splits, strong baselines, uncertainty with calibration, out-of-distribution tests, ablations, limits, open code.'), ('3.8', 'Explain', 'Why might a traffic model trained in one country fail in another?', 'Different vehicles, road users, behaviour, infrastructure and climate shift the data distribution.'), ('3.8', 'Apply', 'How would you show what physics adds to your model?', 'Run an ablation: train the same model without the physics term and compare accuracy and robustness on held-out and out-of-distribution data.')]
